{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9b1c7bd6136e70df","type":"tool_result","content":"artifacts/strat-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9018.md\ndocs/human-review-guide.md\nskills/epic-decompose/prompts/review-agent.md\nskills/epic-decompose/prompts/decompose-agent.md\n---\nCheck: For each epic, verify component and team fields. Look for epics that bundle work across multiple components or teams. **Also check for scope duplication across sibling epics:** compare the scope sections and acceptance criteria of all epics. If two or more epics independently build the same data structure, UI surface, API endpoint, or component (e.g., both create a benchmark list with status indicators, or both implement log viewing against the same endpoint), that is a major issue — it means the boundary was drawn incorrectly and the epics should be merged or re-split along a different axis. Minor incidental overlap (e.g., both epics mention the same config value) is not duplication. **A split that puts two presentation modes of ONE surface in two epics is duplication even though neither epic rebuilds the other's work** — e.g., a P0 epic building a side-by-side comparison view and a P1 epic building a diff view of the same data for the same team: the diff view is a thin increment on the first epic's surface, not an independent deliverable, and the correct shape is ONE epic whose HLR Traceability lists both HLRs. A priority-label difference (P0 vs P1) on the same surface is redundant requirement wording, not a boundary — flag the split as a major issue. **Also check for work-product type mixing:** if a single epic bundles code implementation with content authoring (sample notebooks, tutorials, curated examples), flag as a major issue — these are different kinds of work with different review criteria and should be separate epics even when owned by the same team. **Epic size overages — demand a re-split trigger, not an un-actionable split:** when an epic exceeds the ~2-week guideline but is one logical change — a single mechanical pass over one surface, one audit, where a split would duplicate the audit or fragment the change — do not suggest splitting it. A split demand on that shape produces churn without resolving the overage (the split executes, the epic stays above guideline, and the restructuring can inject new defects). Instead record the overage as a minor issue (watch item) that names a **re-split trigger**: the measurable planning condition under which the epic should be re-split (e.g. \"if the campaign outgrows a sprint of effort, split the rollout work out along environment-setup lines\"). With a trigger on the record — in the decomposition's Health Warnings or in the issue itself — the overage is a managed trade-off and this criterion still scores 2; do not dock the criterion for a triggered, justified overage. An overage acknowledged with no trigger anywhere (a bare \"flagged for planning awareness only\" note) scores 1, with the named correction \"record the concrete re-split trigger\". The carve-out does not apply to genuine boundary violations: an epic spanning multiple sprints of heterogeneous work, or mixing work streams that warrant separate review cycles, is oversized — demand the split (major issue) per the base rubric.\n   - **Work-product type boundaries**: code implementation, content authoring (sample notebooks, tutorials, curated examples), and documentation are different kinds of work — they require different skills, different review criteria, and produce different PR review cycles. An epic that mixes code implementation with content authoring should be split even if the same team owns both. **Exception — incidental documentation touch-ups:** updating captions, screenshots, or references that fall out of a code change (no new content authored) is not a separate work stream — fold it into the originating epic as scope/acceptance criteria rather than a standalone `docs-authoring` epic. Only substantive content/documentation authoring (new tutorials, sample notebooks, curated examples, new or rewritten doc topics), or a strategy with `Documentation Support: Yes`, warrants a separate epic.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8488c55fed33c571","agent_id":"a1241bd17138ca9eb","session_id":"4d2ca8ec-aba3-41c6-ac00-7fc0c2659d24","uuid":"bca49a7a-b90c-4e0e-9361-1731e2a01261","timestamp":"2026-10-25T17:59:51.624Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9b1c7bd6136e70df","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9018"}
