{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-e5aa6a3a-a0b2-47f6-88ce-9f13c582c2cd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-91cc0eb280c35b2c","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9018-_atyc13s/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9018-decomposition.md","content":"# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9018: GPU chargeback reporting for platform administrators\n\nTriage (Step 0): the strategy is NOT below threshold — it affects two components\n(Dashboard, Distributed Workloads) owned by different teams, and its scope spans\narchitecturally distinct work streams: a reporting/data backend with an export\nAPI, a Dashboard admin view, and substantive documentation authoring (HLR-4 is a\nnew doc topic, not an incidental touch-up). It is not documentation-only. Full\ndecomposition follows.\n\n## Step 1 — Strategy structure\n\nParsed from the strategy: High Level Requirements (HLR-1..HLR-4 with\npriorities), Affected Components (Dashboard, Distributed Workloads), Out of\nScope (metering collection pipeline — owned by RHAISTRAT-9990; currency/cost-rate\nmodelling), Open Questions (metering data schema finalization), and a\n`depends-on` link to RHAISTRAT-9990 (GPU usage metering — in flight, separate\nstrategy). No Staff Engineer Input section is present, so no overrides apply.\n\nMissing sections (noted as health warnings, decomposition proceeded with\navailable information): Impacted Teams table, Dependencies table, Given/When/Then\nacceptance criteria, Non-Functional Requirements, and a Technical Approach\nsection. Teams were taken from the architecture-context owning teams.\n\n## Step 2 — Component graph\n\n| Strategy component | Canonical component | Owner team | Classification |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Distributed Workloads | Distributed Workloads | Distributed Workloads | Active — reporting backend, export API, quota data domain |\n| Dashboard | Dashboard | RHOAI Dashboard | Active — administrator-facing consumption-vs-quota view |\n| (documentation deliverable, HLR-4) | Documentation | RHOAI Documentation (assumed — see Health Warnings) | Active — substantive docs authoring |\n\nBoth code components exist in the platform (architecture-context files present:\n`architecture/rhoai-3.0/dashboard.md`, `architecture/rhoai-3.0/distributed-workloads.md`)\nand both are Konflux-onboarded per those files — no repo-onboarding,\nlicense-validation, or konflux-onboarding epics are needed.\n\nComponent mapping notes (non-obvious mappings):\n\n- The strategy lists only \"Dashboard\" and \"Distributed Workloads\" as affected\n  components but assigns no component to the report computation, CSV export, and\n  stable API of HLR-1/HLR-2. That backend work is assigned to **Distributed\n  Workloads**: the Dashboard architecture context states the Dashboard \"owns no\n  data plane\" and exposes \"Kubernetes API only; no private component APIs\", so it\n  cannot host the stable external retrieval API HLR-2 requires. The metering\n  data (delivered by RHAISTRAT-9990) and the Kueue quota model both sit in the\n  Distributed Workloads domain (ClusterQueue/LocalQueue, Kueue metrics exposed to\n  Prometheus per its context file), making it the closest canonical component for\n  the reporting backend.\n- The documentation epic uses the canonical **Documentation** component (present\n  in `.context/rhai-components.txt`). No architecture-context file exists for\n  Documentation.\n\n## Step 3 — Investigation epic evaluation\n\nThe strategy's single open question: the metering data schema finalizes only\nwhen RHAISTRAT-9990 (a separate, in-flight strategy) lands, and the report\nimplementation consuming it carries that external timing risk.\n\nDecision rule applied — does the answer change which downstream epics exist or\nwhat they do? **No.** The reporting backend, Dashboard admin view, and\ndocumentation epics all exist and deliver the same artifacts regardless of the\nschema's final shape; the schema details are discovered at implementation time\nwhen consuming the delivered data. The unknown is resolved by a third party\n(RHAISTRAT-9990), not by technical work inside this strategy. Per Rule 21\n(third party resolves → model as precondition with tiered delivery, no separate\nepic), this is modeled as a **precondition and risk on the reporting epic**, not\nan Investigation epic and not a DAG edge. See Tiered Delivery below.\n\n## Step 4 — HLR-to-epic mapping\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| HLR-1 | P0 | E001 | Per-project GPU-hour reports over selectable time ranges |\n| HLR-2 | P1 | E001 | **bundled** — see below |\n| HLR-3 | P1 | E002 | Dashboard admin view, consumption vs Kueue quota |\n| HLR-4 | P2 | E003 | docs-authoring epic |\n\n**HLR-2 bundling rationale (priority-split analysis):** CSV export and the\nstable retrieval API are delivery modes of the same computed per-project\nGPU-hour report that HLR-1 requires — they share the report data structure and\nAPI surface with the P0 work. Splitting them into a separate P1 epic would\ndouble-build the report computation and add an artificial serialization edge\nbetween two epics building the same surface. HLR-2 is therefore bundled into\nE001 and flagged `bundled` in the HLR Traceability Matrix; it is not separately\nschedulable.\n\n**docs-authoring priority exception:** E003 derives its priority from the\nstrategy frontmatter `priority: Major` → **P1** (not the HLR-4 label P2), and per\nthe exception it does not push its priority onto the implementations it depends\non.\n\n**Priority inheritance:** E001 enables all P0 work (HLR-1) → P0. E002 maps only\nHLR-3 (P1) and blocks no P0 work → P1. E003 is a docs-authoring epic (exempt) →\nP1.\n\n## Epic List\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| RHAISTRAT-9018-E001 | Per-project GPU-hour chargeback reporting backend with CSV export and stable retrieval API | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9018-E002 | Dashboard admin view: current-month GPU consumption against Kueue quota | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9018-E003 | Chargeback reporting documentation: report semantics and export integration | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOI Documentation → corrected: RHOAI Documentation | P1 |\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[\"E001 Reporting backend (P0)\"]\n    E002[\"E002 Dashboard admin view (P1)\"]\n    E003[\"E003 Documentation (P1)\"]\n    E001 --> E002\n    E001 --> E003\n    E002 --> E003\n```\n\nCritical path: E001 → E002 → E003 (length 3).\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| E001 → E002 | Rule 12 (framework/producer → consumer) | The Dashboard admin view builds against the per-project consumption data and retrieval surface the reporting backend produces; the view must not re-implement report computation (the strategy forbids re-implementing the data pipeline). |\n| E001 → E003 | Rule 11 (docs blocked by all Implementations) | Documentation describes report semantics and export integration — it must describe what was built. |\n| E002 → E003 | Rule 11 (docs blocked by all Implementations) | Documentation covers the administrator-facing reporting story, including the Dashboard view's semantics. |\n| (no edge to RHAISTRAT-9990) | Rule 21 (third party resolves → precondition, no separate epic) | The metering schema finalization is external to this strategy and is carried as a precondition/risk on E001, not as an epic or DAG edge. |\n\nNo konflux-onboarding, repo-onboarding, or license-validation epics exist in\nthis decomposition, so Rules 5–10 parallelism constraints do not add edges. Both\nepic-work components are Konflux-onboarded per architecture context.\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Requirement (abridged) | Epic | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| HLR-1 | P0 | Per-project GPU-hour consumption reports over selectable time ranges | E001 | covered |\n| HLR-2 | P1 | CSV export + stable API for finance tooling | E001 | covered — **bundled**: same computed report data structure and retrieval surface as HLR-1; splitting would double-build the report computation and serialize two epics over one surface, so it is not separately schedulable |\n| HLR-3 | P1 | Dashboard admin view: current-month consumption vs Kueue quota | E002 | covered |\n| HLR-4 | P2 | Documentation of report semantics and export integration | E003 | covered |\n\nAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Missing strategy sections (non-blocking):** the strategy has no Impacted\n  Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria,\n  and no Non-Functional Requirements. Teams were inferred from the\n  architecture-context owning teams (RHOAI Dashboard, Distributed Workloads).\n  Epic acceptance criteria were derived from the HLRs since the strategy\n  provides none.\n- **Assumed owning team for documentation:** the canonical Documentation\n  component has no architecture-context file, so no owning team could be\n  verified; RHOAI Documentation is assumed for E003 and should be confirmed at\n  planning.\n- **External dependency timing risk (open question 1):** the metering data\n  schema finalizes only when RHAISTRAT-9990 lands. This is carried as a\n  precondition and risk on E001 (not an epic, not a DAG edge). If E001 starts\n  against a pre-final schema, rework is possible; the risk is tracked in E001's\n  Risks/Notes.\n- **Priority label vs docs exception:** HLR-4 is labelled P2 in the strategy,\n  but E003 is set to P1 per the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy\n  frontmatter priority Major → P1). This is intentional, not an inversion.\n- **Epic sizes:** all three epics are scoped to fit within the ~2-week\n  epic-size guideline; no overage to manage.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nEvaluated per Rule 18 / Step 5.5 for the external dependency with uncertain\ntiming (RHAISTRAT-9990 metering schema finalization):\n\n1. Can the strategy deliver partial value without the finalized metering data?\n   **No.** Every user-facing HLR consumes the metering data: HLR-1/HLR-2 are\n   reports computed from it, and HLR-3's consumption column is computed from it.\n   The only metering-independent sliver (the Kueue quota side of the Dashboard\n   view) carries no standalone user value — an admin view of quotas without\n   consumption is not chargeback reporting.\n2. Therefore: linear dependencies — the external dependency is a **precondition\n   on E001** (all downstream work chains behind it), modeled per Rule 21 as a\n   precondition with no separate epic. No Tier 1/Tier 2 split applies.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":46014,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8488c55fed33c571","agent_id":"a1241bd17138ca9eb","session_id":"4d2ca8ec-aba3-41c6-ac00-7fc0c2659d24","uuid":"31875c85-b517-4ef6-a9e2-1f036f10beca","timestamp":"2026-10-25T18:01:19.731Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9018"}
