val steps are evident; the work is code and API contract delivery. |\n| repo_access | 1 | The Distributed Workloads midstream repos (opendatahub-io/kueue, codeflare-operator, kuberay per the context file) are accessible for clone and modification. |\n| architecture_claims | 1 | The epic's premise is corroborated by `architecture/rhoai-3.0/distributed-workloads.md`: the Kueue ClusterQueue/LocalQueue quota model and Kueue metrics exposed to Prometheus ground both the quota side and the metrics-consumption side of the reporting backend. |\n--- artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9018-E001.md\n---\nepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9018-E001\ntitle: Per-project GPU-hour chargeback reporting backend with CSV export and stable\n  retrieval API\nparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9018\ncomponent: Distributed Workloads\nteam: Distributed Workloads\ntype: Implementation\npriority: P0\nai_signals:\n  change_specificity: 0\n  pattern_precedent: 1\n  adapter_pattern: 0\n  existing_foundation: 0\n  open_questions: -1\n  external_dependency: -1\n  human_process_gates: 0\n  repo_access: 1\n  architecture_claims: 1\nimplementation_type: null\ndependencies: []\ninvestigation_signals: null\nai_implementability: Medium\nai_implementability_score: 1\njira_key: null\nbranch: null\ngated_by: null\ngate_failure_impact: null\n---\n# Per-project GPU-hour chargeback reporting backend with CSV export and stable retrieval API\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic delivers the administrator-facing chargeback reporting backend:\ncomputation of per-project GPU-hour consumption reports over selectable time\nranges, computed from the per-project GPU-hour usage metering data delivered by\nthe in-flight GPU usage metering strategy (RHAISTRAT-9990), plus export of those\nreports as CSV and retrieval via a stable API suitable for finance tooling. The\nreporting layer consumes the metering data; it does not re-implement collection.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Per-project GPU-hour consumption report computation over selectable time\n  ranges (e.g. day, week, month, custom range), aggregating the metering data\n  delivered by the GPU usage metering strategy.\n- A consumption contract against the metering data as delivered by that\n  strategy: the reporting layer reads the data it receives and contains no\n  collection logic of its own.\n- CSV export of the computed reports.\n- A stable, documented retrieval API exposing the computed reports so external\n  finance tooling can pull them programmatically.\n- Quota-related data surfaces needed to support administrator-facing\n  consumption-versus-quota reporting (Kueue ClusterQueue/LocalQueue quota model),\n  consumed read-only.\n\nOut of scope (per strategy):\n\n- The metering collection pipeline itself (owned by RHAISTRAT-9990).\n- Currency/cost-rate modelling — reports are in GPU-hours, not dollars.\n- The Dashboard admin view (separate work stream, owned by the Dashboard team).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given a known metering dataset, the reporting backend returns per-project\n  GPU-hour consumption totals for a selectable time range that match the\n  expected totals computed from that dataset.\n- Given a request for a report over a time range, the response is exportable as\n  CSV and parses correctly in standard spreadsheet tooling.\n- The retrieval API is documented, stable (versioned contract), and returns the\n  same per-project GPU-hour report data as the CSV export.\n- Code review confirms the reporting backend contains no metering collection\n  logic — all usage data originates from the metering data delivered by the GPU\n  usage metering strategy.\n- The report computation is validated against the metering data schema version\n  delivered by the GPU usage metering strategy at the time of implementation.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n| HLR | Priority | Coverage |\n|---|---|---|\n| HLR-1 | P0 | Per-project GPU-hour consumption reports over selectable time ranges, computed from the metering data delivered by RHAISTRAT-9990. |\n| HLR-2 | P1 | Bundled with HLR-1: CSV export and the stable retrieval API are delivery modes of the same computed report — they share the report data structure and retrieval surface with the P0 work, so they are not separately schedulable and stay in this epic. |\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- External timing risk (strategy open question 1): the metering data schema\n  finalizes only when RHAISTRAT-9990 lands. If this epic starts against a\n  pre-final schema, the consumption contract may need rework. This is a\n  precondition carried by this epic (the third-party strategy resolves it); it\n  is a scheduling risk, not a DAG dependency.\n--- artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9018-E002-ai-signals.md\n# AI Implementability Signals — RHAISTRAT-9018-E002\n\nDashboard admin view: current-month GPU consumption against Kueue quota\n(Implementation, RHOAI Dashboard).\n\n| Signal | Value | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| change_specificity | 0 | The view's content is functionally specified (current-month per-project GPU-hour consumption vs Kueue quota, administrator-facing), but no exact routes, files, or field names are given — the data contract comes from the reporting backend delivered earlier in this strategy. |\n| pattern_precedent | 1 | Per `architecture/rhoai-3.0/dashboard.md`, user-facing enablement of components via Dashboard integrations (navigation, resource lists, status views) is a routine, repeated change type in this codebase. |\n| adapter_pattern | 1 | The view follows the Dashboard's established component-integration pattern (read backends via the Kubernetes API surface, add a scoped admin view), an existing reference pattern in the same codebase. |\n| existing_foundation | 1 | Extends the existing Dashboard console and its admin/project views; not greenfield. |\n| open_questions | 0 | The strategy's open question (metering schema finalization) is resolved upstream by the reporting backend this epic consumes; the view builds against that delivered capability and no unresolved design decision changes