Priority\n40\tinheritance is correct: E001 (P0) enables all P0 work; E002 (P1) blocks no P0\n41\twork; E003 is docs-authoring and exempt, correctly set to P1 from the\n42\tstrategy's `priority: Major`. The HLR-2 bundling is the correct shape, not\n43\tpriority collapse: CSV export and the stable retrieval API are delivery modes\n44\tof the same computed report surface HLR-1 requires — a separate P1 epic would\n45\tdouble-build the report computation and serialize two epics over one surface.\n46\tHLR-2 is not a distinct deferrable feature independent of the P0 report.\n47\t\n48\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n49\tNo circular dependencies; all three edges are justified: E001→E002 under\n50\tRule 12 (producer→consumer — the Dashboard view consumes the reporting\n51\tbackend's retrieval surface and must not re-implement computation), and\n52\tE001→E003 / E002→E003 under Rule 11 (docs blocked by all Implementations).\n53\tFrontmatter `dependencies` (E002: [E001]; E003: [E001, E002]) match the summary\n54\tdiagram exactly, with arrows drawn dependency→dependent. No konflux,\n55\trepo-onboarding, or license-validation epics exist, so Rules 5–10 parallelism\n56\tconstraints add no edges; E001 and E002 are different component/team tuples\n57\tcoupled only by the justified data dependency. The decision to model the\n58\tRHAISTRAT-9990 schema finalization as a precondition/risk rather than a DAG\n59\tedge is correct per Rule 21 (third party resolves). Critical path length 3 is\n60\twithin the M-standard heuristic (3–4) for a two-component, four-HLR strategy.\n61\tNo data/artifact dependency discoverable from epic content is missing from the\n62\tDAG.\n63\t\n64\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n65\tEach epic is a distinct component/team tuple (E001 Distributed Workloads /\n66\tDistributed Workloads; E002 Dashboard / RHOAI Dashboard; E003 Documentation /\n67\tRHOAI Documentation), matching the architecture-context owning teams. No\n68\twork-product type mixing — code implementation (E001, E002) is separated from\n69\tcontent authoring (E003, `implementation_type: docs-authoring`). No scope\n70\tduplication: E002 consumes E001's reporting surface rather than rebuilding it,\n71\tand E001 explicitly excludes the Dashboard view. Epic sizes are within the\n72\t~2-week guideline; E001's report computation + CSV + API is one logical change\n73\tover one surface. One minor ambiguity: E001's scope bullet on \"quota-related\n74\tdata surfaces\" loosely overlaps E002, which reads Kueue quota directly via the\n75\tDashboard's existing Kubernetes-API pattern — neither rebuilds the other's\n76\twork, so this is not duplication, but the ownership of quota data surfacing\n77\tcould be stated more precisely.\n78\t\n79\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n80\tAll three epics are Implementation and produce concrete artifacts (reporting\n81\tbackend + API, Dashboard view, published documentation). The decision not to\n82\tcreate an Investigation epic for the metering-schema open question is correct:\n83\tthe outcome is resolved by a third party (RHAISTRAT-9990) and does not change\n84\twhich epics exist or what they do — modeled as a precondition per Rule 21, with\n85\tthe risk carried in E001's Risks/Notes. No `gated_by`/`gate_failure_impact`\n86\tfields are set (nothing gates), and no epic lists an Investigation in\n87\t`dependencies`, so the gating-metadata checks are satisfied vacuously.\n88\t\n89\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n90\tEach epic carries the type-appropriate signal set (`ai_signals` for all three\n91\tImplementation epics; no `investigation_signals` misassignment), and\n92\tai-signals files exist with substantive rationales matching the frontmatter\n93\tvalues. Calibration against `.context` checks out: E001's `architecture_claims:\n94\t1` is corroborated by `architecture/rhoai-3.0/distributed-workloads.md`\n95\t(ClusterQueue/LocalQueue quota model, Kueue metrics exposed to Prometheus), and\n96\tE002's by `dashboard.md` (Kubernetes-API integration pattern, admin surface).\n97\tE003's `architecture_claims: 0` is correct — no architecture-context file\n98\tcovers Documentation. E001's `open_questions: -1` / `external_dependency: -1`\n99\tcorrectly reflect the strategy's open question and the RHAISTRAT-9990\n100\tdepends-on link; E003's `human_process_gates: -1` correctly captures the\n101\tmandatory docs review sign-off. One arguable value: E002's\n102\t`external_dependency: 0` rationale (\"depends only on work inside this\n103\tstrategy\") does not acknowledge the transitive dependence on RHAISTRAT-9990\n104\tmetering data behind the consumption figures it displays — defensible on\n105\tdirect dependencies, but the rationale could note the transitive source.\n106\t\n107\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n108\tEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the HLRs (the strategy provides no\n109\tGiven/When/Then ACs — noted as a health warning). E001's ACs are concrete\n110\t(dataset-match totals, CSV parse in spreadsheet tooling, versioned API contract\n111\treturning the same data as CSV, code-review check for no collection logic).\n112\tE002's ACs are testable (figures match the backend's computed values,\n113\ttraceability via code review). E003 carries the rule-mandated technical-review\n114\tsign-off AC for docs-authoring epics. No replacement epics exist, so\n115\trollback/feature-flag ACs do not apply; no konflux-chain epics exist, so the\n116\tbuild-pipeline AC does not apply. No AC states an externally-controlled\n117\tprecondition — E001's schema-validation AC conditions on the epic's own\n118\tvalidation work against the delivered schema, which is a valid AC. No epic body\n119\treferences sibling epics by draft ID; cross-epic references use descriptive\n120\tphrases (\"the reporting backend\", \"the Dashboard admin view\").\n121\t\n122\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n123\tAll strategy scope is accounted for: report computation with selectable time\n124\tranges, CSV export, stable retrieval API (