{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-98ee7e7e64596e82","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9018\n3\tepic_count: 3\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9018: GPU chargeback reporting for platform administrators\n11\t\n12\tTriage (Step 0): the strategy is NOT below threshold — it affects two components\n13\t(Dashboard, Distributed Workloads) owned by different teams, and its scope spans\n14\tarchitecturally distinct work streams: a reporting/data backend with an export\n15\tAPI, a Dashboard admin view, and substantive documentation authoring (HLR-4 is a\n16\tnew doc topic, not an incidental touch-up). It is not documentation-only. Full\n17\tdecomposition follows.\n18\t\n19\t## Step 1 — Strategy structure\n20\t\n21\tParsed from the strategy: High Level Requirements (HLR-1..HLR-4 with\n22\tpriorities), Affected Components (Dashboard, Distributed Workloads), Out of\n23\tScope (metering collection pipeline — owned by RHAISTRAT-9990; currency/cost-rate\n24\tmodelling), Open Questions (metering data schema finalization), and a\n25\t`depends-on` link to RHAISTRAT-9990 (GPU usage metering — in flight, separate\n26\tstrategy). No Staff Engineer Input section is present, so no overrides apply.\n27\t\n28\tMissing sections (noted as health warnings, decomposition proceeded with\n29\tavailable information): Impacted Teams table, Dependencies table, Given/When/Then\n30\tacceptance criteria, Non-Functional Requirements, and a Technical Approach\n31\tsection. Teams were taken from the architecture-context owning teams.\n32\t\n33\t## Step 2 — Component graph\n34\t\n35\t| Strategy component | Canonical component | Owner team | Classification |\n36\t|---|---|---|---|\n37\t| Distributed Workloads | Distributed Workloads | Distributed Workloads | Active — reporting backend, export API, quota data domain |\n38\t| Dashboard | Dashboard | RHOAI Dashboard | Active — administrator-facing consumption-vs-quota view |\n39\t| (documentation deliverable, HLR-4) | Documentation | RHOAI Documentation (assumed — see Health Warnings) | Active — substantive docs authoring |\n40\t\n41\tBoth code components exist in the platform (architecture-context files present:\n42\t`architecture/rhoai-3.0/dashboard.md`, `architecture/rhoai-3.0/distributed-workloads.md`)\n43\tand both are Konflux-onboarded per those files — no repo-onboarding,\n44\tlicense-validation, or konflux-onboarding epics are needed.\n45\t\n46\tComponent mapping notes (non-obvious mappings):\n47\t\n48\t- The strategy lists only \"Dashboard\" and \"Distributed Workloads\" as affected\n49\t  components but assigns no component to the report computation, CSV export, and\n50\t  stable API of HLR-1/HLR-2. That backend work is assigned to **Distributed\n51\t  Workloads**: the Dashboard architecture context states the Dashboard \"owns no\n52\t  data plane\" and exposes \"Kubernetes API only; no private component APIs\", so it\n53\t  cannot host the stable external retrieval API HLR-2 requires. The metering\n54\t  data (delivered by RHAISTRAT-9990) and the Kueue quota model both sit in the\n55\t  Distributed Workloads domain (ClusterQueue/LocalQueue, Kueue metrics exposed to\n56\t  Prometheus per its context file), making it the closest canonical component for\n57\t  the reporting backend.\n58\t- The documentation epic uses the canonical **Documentation** component (present\n59\t  in `.context/rhai-components.txt`). No architecture-context file exists for\n60\t  Documentation.\n61\t- **Quota-data ownership boundary (revision):** the reporting backend epic\n62\t  (E001) surfaces per-project GPU-hour consumption data only — HLR-1/HLR-2\n63\t  involve no quota data, and its reports do not include a quota column. All\n64\t  Kueue quota surfacing for the administrator view is owned by the Dashboard\n65\t  epic (E002), which reads the ClusterQueue/LocalQueue quota model directly via\n66\t  the Dashboard's existing Kubernetes-API integration pattern. E001's original\n67\t  \"quota-related data surfaces consumed read-only\" scope bullet was dropped in\n68\t  revision to remove this ownership ambiguity.\n69\t\n70\t## Step 3 — Investigation epic evaluation\n71\t\n72\tThe strategy's single open question: the metering data schema finalizes only\n73\twhen RHAISTRAT-9990 (a separate, in-flight strategy) lands, and the report\n74\timplementation consuming it carries that external timing risk.\n75\t\n76\tDecision rule applied — does the answer change which downstream epics exist or\n77\twhat they do? **No.** The reporting backend, Dashboard admin view, and\n78\tdocumentation epics all exist and deliver the same artifacts regardless of the\n79\tschema's final shape; the schema details are discovered at implementation time\n80\twhen consuming the delivered data. The unknown is resolved by a third party\n81\t(RHAISTRAT-9990), not by technical work inside this strategy. Per Rule 21\n82\t(third party resolves → model as precondition with tiered delivery, no separate\n83\tepic), this is modeled as a **precondition and risk on the reporting epic**, not\n84\tan Investigation epic and not a DAG edge. See Tiered Delivery below.\n85\t\n86\t## Step 4 — HLR-to-epic mapping\n87\t\n88\t| HLR | Priority | Epic | Notes |\n89\t|---|---|---|---|\n90\t| HLR-1 | P0 | E001 | Per-project GPU-hour reports over selectable time ranges |\n91\t| HLR-2 | P1 | E001 | **bundled** — see below |\n92\t| HLR-3 | P1 | E002 | Dashboard admin view, consumption vs Kueue quota |\n93\t| HLR-4 | P2 | E003 | docs-authoring epic |\n94\t\n95\t**HLR-2 bundling rationale (priority-split analysis):** CSV export and the\n96\tstable retrieval API are delivery modes of the same computed per-project\n97\tGPU-hour report that HLR-1 requires — they share the report data structure and\n98\tAPI surface with the P0 work. Splitting them into a separate P1 epic would\n99\tdouble-build the report computation and add an artificial serialization edge\n100\tbetween two epics building the same surface. HLR-2 is therefore bundled into\n101\tE001 and flagged `bundled` in the HLR Traceability Matrix; it is not separately\n102\tschedulable.\n103\t\n104\t**docs-authoring priority exception:** E003 derives its priority from the\n105\tstrategy frontmatter `priority: Major` → **P1** (not the HLR-4 label P2), and per\n106\tthe exception it does not push its priority onto the implementations it depends\n107\ton.\n108\t\n109\t**Priority inheritance:** E001 enables all P0 work (HLR-1) → P0. E002 maps only\n110\tHLR-3 (P1) and blocks no P0 work → P1. E003 is a docs-authoring epic (exempt) →\n111\tP1.\n112\t\n113\t## Epic List\n114\t\n115\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n116\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n117\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E001 | Per-project GPU-hour chargeback reporting backend with CSV export and stable retrieval API | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P0 |\n118\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E002 | Dashboard admin view: current-month GPU consumption against Kueue quota | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n119\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E003 | Chargeback reporting documentation: report semantics and export integration | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Documentation | P1 |\n120\t\n121\t## Dependency DAG\n122\t\n123\t```mermaid\n124\tgraph TD\n125\t    E001[\"E001 Reporting backend (P0)\"]\n126\t    E002[\"E002 Dashboard admin view (P1)\"]\n127\t    E003[\"E003 Documentation (P1)\"]\n128\t    E001 --> E002\n129\t    E001 --> E003\n130\t    E002 --> E003\n131\t```\n132\t\n133\tCritical path: E001 → E002 → E003 (length 3).\n134\t\n135\t## DAG Justification\n136\t\n137\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n138\t|---|---|---|\n139\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 12 (framework/producer → consumer) | The Dashboard admin view builds against the per-project consumption data and retrieval surface the reporting backend produces; the view must not re-implement report computation (the strategy forbids re-implementing the data pipeline). |\n140\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 11 (docs blocked by all Implementations) | Documentation describes report semantics and export integration — it must describe what was built. |\n141\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 11 (docs blocked by all Implementations) | Documentation covers the administrator-facing reporting story, including the Dashboard view's semantics. |\n142\t| (no edge to RHAISTRAT-9990) | Rule 21 (third party resolves → precondition, no separate epic) | The metering schema finalization is external to this strategy and is carried as a precondition/risk on E001, not as an epic or DAG edge. |\n143\t\n144\tNo konflux-onboarding, repo-onboarding, or license-validation epics exist in\n145\tthis decomposition, so Rules 5–10 parallelism constraints do not add edges. Both\n146\tepic-work components are Konflux-onboarded per architecture context.\n147\t\n148\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n149\t\n150\t| HLR | Priority | Requirement (abridged) | Epic | Status |\n151\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n152\t| HLR-1 | P0 | Per-project GPU-hour consumption reports over selectable time ranges | E001 | covered |\n153\t| HLR-2 | P1 | CSV export + stable API for finance tooling | E001 | covered — **bundled**: same computed report data structure and retrieval surface as HLR-1; splitting would double-build the report computation and serialize two epics over one surface, so it is not separately schedulable |\n154\t| HLR-3 | P1 | Dashboard admin view: current-month consumption vs Kueue quota | E002 | covered |\n155\t| HLR-4 | P2 | Documentation of report semantics and export integration | E003 | covered |\n156\t\n157\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n158\t\n159\t## Health Warnings\n160\t\n161\t- **Missing strategy sections (non-blocking):** the strategy has no Impacted\n162\t  Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria,\n163\t  and no Non-Functional Requirements. Te