{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9310aaf78f93b0ea","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9018-E001\n3\ttitle: Per-project GPU-hour chargeback reporting backend with CSV export and stable\n4\t  retrieval API\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9018\n6\tcomponent: Distributed Workloads\n7\tteam: Distributed Workloads\n8\ttype: Implementation\n9\tpriority: P0\n10\tai_signals:\n11\t  change_specificity: 0\n12\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n13\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n14\t  existing_foundation: 0\n15\t  open_questions: -1\n16\t  external_dependency: -1\n17\t  human_process_gates: 0\n18\t  repo_access: 1\n19\t  architecture_claims: 1\n20\timplementation_type: null\n21\tdependencies: []\n22\tinvestigation_signals: null\n23\tai_implementability: Medium\n24\tai_implementability_score: 1\n25\tjira_key: null\n26\tbranch: null\n27\tgated_by: null\n28\tgate_failure_impact: null\n29\t---\n30\t# Per-project GPU-hour chargeback reporting backend with CSV export and stable retrieval API\n31\t\n32\t## Description\n33\t\n34\tThis epic delivers the administrator-facing chargeback reporting backend:\n35\tcomputation of per-project GPU-hour consumption reports over selectable time\n36\tranges, computed from the per-project GPU-hour usage metering data delivered by\n37\tthe in-flight GPU usage metering strategy (RHAISTRAT-9990), plus export of those\n38\treports as CSV and retrieval via a stable API suitable for finance tooling. The\n39\treporting layer consumes the metering data; it does not re-implement collection.\n40\t\n41\t## Scope\n42\t\n43\t- Per-project GPU-hour consumption report computation over selectable time\n44\t  ranges (e.g. day, week, month, custom range), aggregating the metering data\n45\t  delivered by the GPU usage metering strategy.\n46\t- A consumption contract against the metering data as delivered by that\n47\t  strategy: the reporting layer reads the data it receives and contains no\n48\t  collection logic of its own.\n49\t- CSV export of the computed reports.\n50\t- A stable, documented retrieval API exposing the computed reports so external\n51\t  finance tooling can pull them programmatically.\n52\t\n53\tOut of scope (per strategy):\n54\t\n55\t- The metering collection pipeline itself (owned by RHAISTRAT-9990).\n56\t- Currency/cost-rate modelling — reports are in GPU-hours, not dollars.\n57\t- The Dashboard admin view (separate work stream, owned by the Dashboard team).\n58\t- Surfacing Kueue quota data for the administrator view: the reports delivered\n59\t  here cover per-project GPU-hour consumption only (HLR-1/HLR-2 involve no\n60\t  quota data). The Dashboard team's view reads the Kueue quota model\n61\t  (ClusterQueue/LocalQueue) directly via the Dashboard's existing\n62\t  Kubernetes-API integration pattern, so no quota surface is exposed by this\n63\t  epic.\n64\t\n65\t## Acceptance Criteria\n66\t\n67\t- Given a known metering dataset, the reporting backend returns per-project\n68\t  GPU-hour consumption totals for a selectable time range that match the\n69\t  expected totals computed from that dataset.\n70\t- Given a request for a report over a time range, the response is exportable as\n71\t  CSV and parses correctly in standard spreadsheet tooling.\n72\t- The retrieval API is documented, stable (versioned contract), and returns the\n73\t  same per-project GPU-hour report data as the CSV export.\n74\t- Code review confirms the reporting backend contains no metering collection\n75\t  logic — all usage data originates from the metering data delivered by the GPU\n76\t  usage metering strategy.\n77\t- The report computation is validated against the metering data schema version\n78\t  delivered by the GPU usage metering strategy at the time of implementation.\n79\t\n80\t## HLR Traceability\n81\t\n82\t| HLR | Priority | Coverage |\n83\t|---|---|---|\n84\t| HLR-1 | P0 | Per-project GPU-hour consumption reports over selectable time ranges, computed from the metering data delivered by RHAISTRAT-9990. |\n85\t| HLR-2 | P1 | Bundled with HLR-1: CSV export and the stable retrieval API are delivery modes of the same computed report — they share the report data structure and retrieval surface with the P0 work, so they are not separately schedulable and stay in this epic. |\n86\t\n87\t## Risks / Notes\n88\t\n89\t- External timing risk (strategy open question 1): the metering data schema\n90\t  finalizes only when RHAISTRAT-9990 lands. If this epic starts against a\n91\t  pre-final schema, the consumption contract may need rework. This is a\n92\t  precondition carried by this epic (the third-party strategy resolves it); it\n93\t  is a scheduling risk, not a DAG dependency.\n94\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a0aab55906adba82","agent_id":"af0f2ce9af0189870","session_id":"4d2ca8ec-aba3-41c6-ac00-7fc0c2659d24","uuid":"119c4ff1-477c-4d6b-b9b3-9949f704f2c1","timestamp":"2026-10-25T18:08:16.799Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9310aaf78f93b0ea","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review revised decomposition"}
